The result is that gradients calculated via the continuous adjoint method will not be as accurate as those computed by backpropagating through the solver. (Which are the gold standard, corresponding to the model actually used.) This means that training may be slower, final model performance may be impacted, and in the worst case training may fail altogether. [GKB19] give a description of the possible failure modes of the continuous adjoint method, and [OR20] perform a thorough empirical investigation comparing optimise-then-discretise against discretise-then-optimise, in favour the of the latter.
